{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 6,
  "simulation_id": "sim-03",
  "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B030",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B006",
    "B001"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks meet a particularly demanding experimental standard: magic-wavelength confinement permits many atoms to contribute while protecting the clock transition, and 10^-18-class performance has been demonstrated independently. This is not simply an incremental accuracy record but a robust new measurement architecture that makes frequency comparison a practical probe of time, gravitation, and possible changes in fundamental constants. Katori's originating lattice-clock concept and Ye's decisive experimental development form a compact, complementary, and experimentally mature attribution.",
    "case_against": "The clocks are principally an enabling measurement capability, not yet a completed landmark discovery of new fundamental physics. The historical boundary also requires care: the award must recognize the lattice-clock architecture and its realization without absorbing the broader development of optical-frequency metrology.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson argues that WMAP supplied a completed empirical transformation of cosmology, whereas the most consequential discoveries enabled by lattice clocks may still be prospective.",
        "response": "WMAP is a compelling completed observational achievement, and its direct cosmological consequences are stronger than any particular result so far obtained with a lattice clock. I nevertheless retain B030 because a reproducible instrument can itself be prize-worthy when it changes the attainable experimental regime. The relevant maturity test is whether the physical principle works reliably across laboratories and opens measurements previously inaccessible; the supplied record indicates that it does."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson favors circuit QED as a mature, broadly reproduced controllable quantum-optical architecture, while recognizing that its credit line spans a broad lineage.",
        "response": "I agree that circuit QED established an important controllable regime and that its experimental evidence is substantial. But its proposed achievement combines theory, architecture, and a wider superconducting-circuit history, making a three-person boundary difficult. B030 more sharply connects a physical principle, reproducible realization, and two complementary contributors, while avoiding claims about unfinished quantum-computing applications."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth emphasizes that independent 10^-18-level agreement is the evidence that distinguishes lattice clocks from an impressive isolated demonstration, and urges a citation confined to the clock itself.",
        "response": "I concur. Independent reproduction is decisive for an experimental prize of this kind: it demonstrates that the central architecture is robust rather than laboratory-specific. Her proposed citation discipline also resolves the principal maturity concern by rewarding the invention and development of optical lattice clocks, not hypothetical future discoveries."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical documentation can delimit Katori's original lattice-clock contribution and Ye's decisive developments from the wider optical-frequency-metrology lineage with sufficient precision.",
      "Whether the committee should instead prefer an achievement with a completed observational or conceptual transformation, despite B030's exceptional experimental maturity and attribution.",
      "Whether the citation should mention magic-wavelength confinement explicitly or remain at the broader level of invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No apparent connection to Katori or Ye is supplied. My WACQT leadership overlaps institutionally with Göran Johansson's Chalmers environment, but no specific collaboration or conflict concerning the preferred achievement is identified."
  }
}
